      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Smart Gyms</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these systems. They were chosen from manufacturer product pages,
            installation requirements, warranty and membership terms, recall records and independent
            coverage, and the claims on this page are limited to what those sources support. Each pick
            had to be on sale on its maker's US store on 19 October 2026, and its basic figures —
            resistance range, size, warranty and the cost of the membership — had to agree between the
            maker and at least one independent reviewer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recall checks.</strong> We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall
            database by brand name, by manufacturer and in recall descriptions for every maker on this
            page — Tonal, Speediance, Beyond Power and Tempo — and for the brands we considered and left
            out, including Amp, Vitruvian and BowFlex. We found no recalls for Tonal, Speediance or Beyond
            Power. The only Tempo-named recall on record is for kitchen pressure cookers sold by an
            unrelated company. BowFlex's recent recalls are its SelectTech adjustable dumbbells (June
            2025) and SelectTech barbells (2021), neither of which is a smart gym.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What we left out, and why</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Amp.</strong> Amp is a wall-mounted smart cable machine that uses your iPhone or iPad
            as its screen and has been covered favourably as a cheaper Tonal rival. In December 2025 the
            CPSC announced a recall of about 1,900 AMP MP2 Smart Fitness Machines, sold on Amp's website
            from January to August 2025 for about $1,800, because the arm can fail to lock and swing
            unexpectedly, posing a laceration and serious injury risk; owners were told to stop using it
            and book a free in-home repair. Amp's current pages do not make clear to us which hardware
            revision new orders receive, so it stays off the list. If you already own one, check the
            recall notice and confirm the repair has been done.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vitruvian Trainer+.</strong> The Trainer+ is a floor-standing digital platform that
            often appears in Tonal comparisons. On 19
            October 2026 Vitruvian's own store listed only an accessory kit, and its home page carried a
            holding message rather than the machine, so we could not confirm it as currently sold.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>BowFlex.</strong> BowFlex's current home gyms, the Revolution (SpiraFlex resistance)
            and the Xtreme 2 SE (Power Rod resistance), are traditional machines without motors or
            connected coaching. They can be good buys, but they are not smart gyms; our
            <a href="/best-home-gym-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym machines guide</a>
            covers that category.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Newer Speediance machines.</strong> Speediance began selling the Gym Monster 3 and 3S
            in August 2026 and the rack-mountable Gym Nano in October 2026. Speediance lists the Gym
            Monster 3 at 220 lb and the 3S at 260 lb, and the Gym Nano at 220 lb from an 18 lb unit, but
            we found no independent coverage of either yet, so they are mentioned here rather than carded.
            The Gym Monster 2 remains on sale alongside them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Fitness mirrors and app-only systems.</strong> Mirrors stream classes but add no
            resistance, and app-only coaching works with equipment you already own; neither is what most
            people mean by a smart home gym, so we left them out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="digital-resistance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How Digital Resistance Works, and What the Numbers Mean</h2>
<p>
            Three of the five picks use digital resistance: an electric motor winds a cable, and software
            sets how hard it pulls back. There is no stack of plates, so the load can change between reps,
            or within a rep, without you touching anything. That is the real advantage of the category,
            and it is why the quoted maximum is only part of the story.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Maximum load is split between arms</h3>
          <p>
            A machine with two arms or two cables usually quotes its total. Tonal's 250 lb is 125 lb per
            arm, and Speediance's 220 lb is 110 lb per side, as their reviewers describe. That matters for
            single-arm work and for movements where you hold both handles together: a single-arm row on a
            two-motor machine tops out at the per-side figure. The Voltra I has one cable and quotes 5 to
            200 lb for it, so a single-cable movement can use the whole figure.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is 200 or 250 pounds enough?</h3>
          <p>
            For most people training for health and general strength, yes. Upper-body pulling and pressing
            movements, single-leg work and accessory lifts rarely need more. Where digital machines run out
            is the heavy two-legged lifts: a trained lifter's squat, deadlift or hip thrust can exceed what
            any of these produce, and the cable geometry of a wall unit is not the same as a barbell on
            your back. Garage Gym Reviews flags the maximum on both the Tonal and the Speediance as a limit for
            stronger users. If those lifts are central to your training, a
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">power rack</a>
            with a barbell is still the tool, and a rack-mounted unit like the Voltra can sit on it for
            cable work.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Modes beyond a constant weight</h3>
          <p>
            Because the motor controls the cable, digital machines offer modes a weight stack cannot:
            heavier lowering phases (eccentric loading), a band-like curve that gets harder as the cable
            extends, a "chain" mode that adds load toward the top of a lift, and spotter features that
            ease the weight when you slow down. Speediance lists Standard, Chain, Eccentric, Fixed Speed
            and Sled modes for its newest unit; Beyond Power describes a resistance band mode and a damper
            mode on the Voltra; Tonal ties its smart features to its membership. These are genuinely
            useful for progression and for training alone, but they are software features, and on Tonal
            most of them sit behind the subscription.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Free weights with tracking</h3>
          <p>
            Tempo takes the opposite approach. You lift ordinary colour-coded dumbbells and plates, and the
            Tempo app on an iPhone watches you through the phone's camera, counts reps, identifies the
            weight and gives form cues. There is no motor, so no adaptive load, but there is also nothing
            to fail electrically and nothing limiting you except the plates you own. The trade-off is the
            maximum: the Starter Bundle tops out at 50 lb, according to Garage Gym Reviews, and heavier
            packages cost more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wall-vs-freestanding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Wall-Mounted vs Freestanding: What Your Room Needs</h2>
<p>
            The choice between a wall unit and a floor unit is mostly decided by your walls, your floor and
            whether you own the home.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wall-mounted (Tonal)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Studs or concrete.</strong> Tonal says the unit installs much like a TV bracket and
            works with most wood and metal studs 16 to 24 inches apart; our cable machine guide also notes
            installation onto solid concrete. Plaster-over-lath walls, unusual stud spacing and walls with
            pipes or wiring in the wrong place are things to raise before you order.</li>
            <li><strong>Height and floor.</strong> Tonal gives 7 ft 10 in as the ideal ceiling height for the
            arms to extend fully and says the unit can still work with a 7 ft ceiling. Our earlier check of
            Tonal and Garage Gym Reviews gave 7 ft of unobstructed wall and a 7 x 7 ft floor area in front.</li>
            <li><strong>Power and Wi-Fi.</strong> Tonal asks for continuous, reliable Wi-Fi and a grounded,
            three-prong outlet within 6 feet of the location, with no extension cord.</li>
            <li><strong>Installation.</strong> Tonal's own team delivers and mounts the unit. On 19 October
            delivery and installation started at $295 depending on location, and Tonal listed a $300 fee to
            detach and reinstall in the same home.</li>
            <li><strong>Renting.</strong> Drilling into studs leaves holes, and a lease may forbid it. Ask the
            landlord in writing first, and keep the answer.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Freestanding (Speediance)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>No drilling.</strong> The Gym Monster 2 stands on its own base, and Speediance says it
            needs no drilling or wall mounting, which is why it is the usual answer for renters.</li>
            <li><strong>Floor space and weight.</strong> It occupies about 48 x 27 in unfolded and folds to
            about 14.5 in deep, but the unit weighs about 172 lb and PCWorld describes it arriving in three
            packages totalling just under 353 lb. Speediance sells a separate in-home delivery service,
            priced from $150 for ground-floor delivery on 19 October. Check that your floor and stairs suit it,
            and see our <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a>
            if it is going on a hard floor.</li>
            <li><strong>Power.</strong> Like any motorised machine, plug it straight into a wall outlet; check
            Speediance's manual for its electrical requirements before choosing a spot.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Rack-mounted and portable (Voltra, Tempo)</h3>
          <p>
            The Voltra I clamps to a power rack, a wall-mounted upright or a travel platform using mounts
            sold separately, and runs on a rechargeable battery; Beyond Power quotes a 97.9 Wh battery. It
            needs a rack, or a mount, that can take the load you will pull. Tempo Move needs the least: a
            small cabinet, a TV to cast to, and a clear training area in front of the camera — Garage Gym
            Reviews suggests about 6 x 6 ft. If you do not have a rack yet, our
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat rack guide</a>
            covers footprint and anchoring, and folding racks are a sibling guide in this series.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What the Membership Really Costs</h2>
<p>
            The screen price is rarely the price. The table below adds three years of each system's
            membership to the hardware price we saw on 19 October 2026. It is arithmetic from those stated
            inputs, not a quote: it leaves out tax, financing, promotions and price changes, and the
            membership prices themselves change.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">System</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Hardware (19 Oct)</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Membership</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">3-year total (arithmetic)</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tonal 2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,295</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$59.95/mo, 12-month commitment</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$6,453 ($4,295 + 36 x $59.95), before accessories and installation</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tonal 1 Certified Refurbished</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,495</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$59.95/mo, 12-month commitment</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,653, before accessories and installation</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speediance Gym Monster 2 (Basic)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,387</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Optional Wellness+; $149.99/yr on Speediance's store</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,387 without it; $3,837 with three annual plans</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Beyond Power Voltra I</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,199 + mount</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,368 to $2,458 with a $169 to $259 rack mount</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tempo Move (Starter)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$708 incl. first 12 months</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$39/mo after year one</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,644 ($708 + 24 x $39)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Tonal.</strong> Tonal's product page says each purchase requires a 12-month membership
            commitment at $59.95 a month plus tax, that an active membership is required for most features,
            and that activation happens after installation. Garage Gym Reviews gives the same $59.95. Tonal
            also says its Smart Accessories, sold as $495 and $595 bundles on 19 October, are required to
            unlock the machine's full functionality, and delivery and installation start at $295. Add those
            and a Tonal 2 crosses $7,000 over three years. Tonal also offers a rental programme run by a
            third party; read its terms closely, because it is described as not rent-to-own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Speediance.</strong> On 19 October Speediance's store sold Wellness+ at $19.99 a month or
            $149.99 a year, both shown as discounts from higher list prices. Garage Gym Reviews' roundup,
            which covers the original Gym Monster, lists a $29-a-month subscription, so the price has
            moved or differs by model; check it at checkout. PCWorld, cited on our
            cable machine page, says the machine works without the subscription for classic strength
            training but that some functions need it. That makes the Gym Monster 2 the cheapest digital
            machine here to keep running if you are happy with the free features.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Voltra and Tempo.</strong> Garage Gym Reviews notes that the Voltra's app has no
            subscription fee, so the cost is the unit and its mounts. Tempo bundles the first year of
            training into the hardware price; Garage Gym Reviews describes that as $59 a month for the first
            12 months and $39 a month after, which matches the $708 Starter Bundle price on Tempo's store.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more cost to weigh is what happens when you stop paying. A Tonal without a membership loses
            most of its features, by Tonal's own terms; a Speediance or Voltra keeps working as a cable
            machine; Tempo's dumbbells stay dumbbells. If the company changes its terms or its servers,
            the machines that do the most in the app have the most to lose.
          </p>
