        <div id="buyers-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Smart Home Gym Buyer's Guide</h2>
<p>
            Work through these in order. Most regret with smart gyms comes from skipping one of the first
            three.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Decide whether you want a coach or a machine.</strong> If you mainly want guided
            programmes that change the weight for you, pay for the platform with the best content and
            accept the membership. If you mainly want cable resistance, a freestanding or rack-mounted
            unit without a mandatory fee, or an iron
            <a href="/best-cable-machines-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cable machine</a>,
            costs less over time.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the wall or the floor before the colour.</strong> For a wall unit, find the
            studs and measure the ceiling. For a freestanding unit, measure the unfolded footprint plus room
            to move, and plan the delivery route for a heavy crate.</li>
            <li><strong>Price three years, not one.</strong> Use the table above with current prices. A
            cheaper machine with a dearer membership can overtake a dearer machine within two years.</li>
            <li><strong>Match the maximum to your heaviest lift.</strong> Think about rows, squats and
            hinges, not curls, and remember that two-arm machines split their maximum between arms.</li>
            <li><strong>Read the warranty and the add-ons.</strong> Tonal and Speediance both include two
            years on the main unit and sell extended cover; Beyond Power lists a one-year warranty, per
            Garage Gym Reviews, with paid care plans. Accessories you will need — handles, bars, benches,
            mounts — are often extra.</li>
            <li><strong>Think about a bench and a floor.</strong> Most programmes assume a bench. Our
            <a href="/best-weight-benches/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight bench guide</a>
            and <a href="/best-home-gym-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym accessories guide</a>
            cover both, and many people pair a smart unit with a set of
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbells</a>
            for carries and warm-ups.</li>
            <li><strong>Plan for cardio separately.</strong> None of these replaces a bike or treadmill for
            conditioning. If you want screen-led cardio too, our
            <a href="/best-peloton-alternatives/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton alternatives guide</a>
            and <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home cardio machines guide</a>
            pick up from here.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best smart home gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people who can wall-mount, the Tonal 2: 5 to 250 lb of digital resistance, camera form feedback and professional installation, at $4,295 plus a required $59.95-a-month membership. If you cannot drill, the freestanding Speediance Gym Monster 2 is the better choice, and its subscription is optional.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a smart home gym cost per month?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 19 October 2026, Tonal's membership was $59.95 a month with a 12-month commitment; Speediance's optional Wellness+ was $19.99 a month or $149.99 a year on its store; Tempo charged $39 a month after the first year; and the Beyond Power Voltra app had no subscription fee. Prices change, so check before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use Tonal without a membership?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only in a limited way. Tonal says each purchase requires a 12-month membership commitment and that an active membership is required for most features. If you want a digital machine that keeps most of its use without a subscription, the Speediance Gym Monster 2 or the Voltra I are better fits.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a Tonal need to be mounted to studs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Tonal says it installs much like a TV bracket on most wood and metal studs 16 to 24 inches apart, and its team does the installation. It also needs a grounded three-prong outlet within 6 feet, steady Wi-Fi, and ideally 7 feet 10 inches of ceiling, though Tonal says it can work with 7 feet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best smart home gym for renters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A freestanding or portable system. The Speediance Gym Monster 2 needs no drilling and folds against a wall, and Tempo Move is a small cabinet of dumbbells. The Voltra I needs a rack or mount rather than a wall. If you want a Tonal, get the landlord's written permission before installation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a smart home gym worth it compared with weights?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it if you will use the coaching and value the space saving and automatic load changes. It is poor value if you mainly want heavy squats and deadlifts, or will drop the membership after a few months. A rack, barbell and plates cost less over time and have no ceiling on load.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Smart Home Gym</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and expensive to fix after installation.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pricing the hardware and forgetting the membership</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A smart gym with a required membership costs far more over three years than its sticker. Add the monthly fee for 36 months, the accessories the maker says you need, and delivery or installation, then compare that total with the alternatives. Do the sum with the prices on the day you buy, not the ones in a review.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering a wall unit before checking the wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Wall-mounted machines need studs at the right spacing, enough ceiling height and an outlet nearby without an extension cord. Find the studs, measure the ceiling and check the outlet before you order, and get a landlord's permission in writing if you rent. A failed installation survey can delay delivery by weeks.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for curls when your heaviest lift is a squat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 200 or 250 lb maximum sounds like plenty until you split it between two arms and try to load a squat or hip thrust. Pick a system for your heaviest lower-body movement, or plan to keep a barbell and rack for those lifts and use the smart unit for everything else.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a used smart gym without a recall check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Secondhand smart gyms are common, and at least one recent model, the AMP MP2, has been recalled for an arm that can fail to lock. Get the model and serial number, search the CPSC recall database, and confirm with the maker that any required repair has been done and that the membership can transfer to you.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

